{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2364 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "903b7261cdf700feb851e5c1abef0430ac4d68e0fb4481799bc16c6a7c38af66" :edb/db-before-t 2363 :edb/db-after-t 2364 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535676 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:56:00.630Z" 13194139535676 true] [17592186152217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360637 13194139535676 true] [17592186152217 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360664 13194139535676 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360671 13194139535676 true] [17592186152218 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360678 13194139535676 true] [17592186152219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360644 13194139535676 true] [17592186360637 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360638 13194139535676 true] [17592186360637 1089 "q-129957" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360637 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535676 true] [17592186360637 1100 "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{4t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." 13194139535676 true] [17592186360637 1113 #uuid "f2fac4e5-7135-5e39-8138-1f3b4b97226e" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360637 1145 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{4t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+4u{e}^{4t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t})-16u{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}({u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+8w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-8$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-8t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-8\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}.\n$$" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360639 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360640 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360641 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360642 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360643 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1041 "selection" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360642 13194139535676 true] [17592186360638 1110 #uuid "a3f33fb8-e98b-5cf5-92e4-d3998c8c60fa" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360639 1034 "y=A{e}^{4t}+Bt{e}^{4t}" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360639 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535676 true] [17592186360639 1111 #uuid "aae460c7-0e1f-569b-bec4-226c4f1ef6fb" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360640 1034 "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{t}" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535676 true] [17592186360640 1111 #uuid "84655674-afa3-5a63-8c65-2e82b4512494" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360641 1034 "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{t}^{4}" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535676 true] [17592186360641 1111 #uuid "e39dbe18-f115-5185-bfda-4452826d62e5" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360642 1034 "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535676 true] [17592186360642 1111 #uuid "c575c40a-3b6b-590b-8f58-bd27f16719c7" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360643 1034 "y=A{e}^{4t}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360643 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535676 true] [17592186360643 1111 #uuid "fdc8d2dc-caa5-54f8-86f7-dba73b353657" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360645 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360648 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360651 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360656 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360661 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1089 "q-339879" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1100 "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=-12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1113 #uuid "703a7a55-d89b-5466-a847-c7db23ef2a30" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360644 1145 "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-2x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-2{e}^{-2x})^{′}=4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(4{e}^{-2x})+5(-2{e}^{-2x})-6({e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-2x})^{′}={e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})^{′}=-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x})+5({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})-6(x{e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." 13194139535676 true] [17592186360645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360646 13194139535676 true] [17592186360645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360647 13194139535676 true] [17592186360645 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535676 true] [17592186360645 1041 "field-1" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360645 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360647 13194139535676 true] [17592186360645 1110 #uuid "34067bab-9188-5585-b369-e90b291a5a61" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360646 1034 "isn't" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360646 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535676 true] [17592186360646 1111 #uuid "402446f4-6bf7-54bf-9b23-826e89be6924" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360647 1034 "is" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535676 true] [17592186360647 1111 #uuid "b75153cf-90d4-5a3e-bded-2f36ba1f87f9" 13194139535676 true] [17592186360648 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360649 13194139535676 true] [17592186360648 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360650 13